ure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-db25a48d-03e1-551c-b686-ab08530dbada" :step/id #uuid "db25a48d-03e1-551c-b686-ab08530dbada" :step/content 17592186326895 :step/next "step-8a4eb61b-cd4e-51aa-a92c-b2f72c08f03f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8a4eb61b-cd4e-51aa-a92c-b2f72c08f03f" :step/id #uuid "8a4eb61b-cd4e-51aa-a92c-b2f72c08f03f" :step/content 17592186326896 :step/next "step-d8e8a02e-385c-546e-a7e7-66e3e6ee2675" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d8e8a02e-385c-546e-a7e7-66e3e6ee2675" :step/id #uuid "d8e8a02e-385c-546e-a7e7-66e3e6ee2675" :step/content 17592186134372 :step/next "step-a7457c42-668c-5326-bdf6-c03519363ce9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a7457c42-668c-5326-bdf6-c03519363ce9" :step/id #uuid "a7457c42-668c-5326-bdf6-c03519363ce9" :step/content 17592186134374 :step/next "step-3b38d3ba-b3fc-5a7a-a4cc-f7a07636c5f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3b38d3ba-b3fc-5a7a-a4cc-f7a07636c5f2" :step/id #uuid "3b38d3ba-b3fc-5a7a-a4cc-f7a07636c5f2" :step/content 17592186134105 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186169095 :answer-field/choices 17592186169096] [:db/retract 17592186169095 :answer-field/choices 17592186169097] [:db/retract 17592186169095 :answer-field/choices 17592186169098] [:db/retract 17592186169095 :answer-field/choices 17592186169099] [:db/retract 17592186169095 :answer-field/choices 17592186169100] [:db/retract 17592186168885 :answer-field/choices 17592186168886] [:db/retract 17592186168885 :answer-field/choices 17592186168887] [:db/retract 17592186168885 :answer-field/choices 17592186168888] [:db/retract 17592186168885 :answer-field/choices 17592186168889] [:db/retract 17592186168885 :answer-field/choices 17592186168890] {:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b0fde5c-0530-5337-9e62-63e3ac799868" "question-42368253-8090-5197-973b-1010d704c08f" "question-9a0fa4ee-d860-5a97-91a9-24370dbc9085" "question-f28f1c0e-4ffd-5dee-98b7-1c475d5129ec" "question-c51957e8-149e-57cd-8ee2-fb199eb311cd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171688 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7  x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] =  0.7 \\pm 0.3\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-764e52d5-673e-5136-a2ff-4fbb20c5aad9" "question-c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" "question-939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171696 :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they finally obtained on that exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b0fde5c-0530-5337-9e62-63e3ac799868" :question/id #uuid "4b0fde5c-0530-5337-9e62-63e3ac799868" :question/math-academy-id "q-159393" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{11},{y}_{11})$ of $n=11$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.78,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=110,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.262)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $9$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1-2.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-2.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.262)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{9,0.025}=2.262.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.262\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.78}{110}}≈0.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-2.6\\pm 0.2\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f561bd09-f9f3-5087-9384-dc94b699a804"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f561bd09-f9f3-5087-9384-dc94b699a804" :answer-field/id #uuid "f561bd09-f9f3-5087-9384-dc94b699a804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44edb47e-bcc6-56b2-a80f-28051a0a9b01" "answer-2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" "answer-5bb3fbf6-a548-535d-b241-e4dfd3a43153" "answer-e596f17d-fc27-5a9d-baa8-083f85f564e5" "answer-bd62c1f0-f5e8-5348-b782-6e34e684508d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44edb47e-bcc6-56b2-a80f-28051a0a9b01" :answer/id #uuid "44edb47e-bcc6-56b2-a80f-28051a0a9b01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02d