le.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1.5+3.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=3.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.746)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.05}=1.746.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.746\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2.53}{484}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=3.8\\pm 0.1\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" "answer-c637d326-e1bb-5f3b-830f-2255667712ef" "answer-e1a2f733-69ad-5912-bdd1-d5b4f9a00a90" "answer-57ca5bd7-3818-541b-9864-07b885eecc3b" "answer-88714f63-0f79-5490-96fc-316d04ce4e71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/id #uuid "35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c637d326-e1bb-5f3b-830f-2255667712ef" :answer/id #uuid "c637d326-e1bb-5f3b-830f-2255667712ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1a2f733-69ad-5912-bdd1-d5b4f9a00a90" :answer/id #uuid "e1a2f733-69ad-5912-bdd1-d5b4f9a00a90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57ca5bd7-3818-541b-9864-07b885eecc3b" :answer/id #uuid "57ca5bd7-3818-541b-9864-07b885eecc3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88714f63-0f79-5490-96fc-316d04ce4e71" :answer/id #uuid "88714f63-0f79-5490-96fc-316d04ce4e71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\pm 1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172372 :question/problem "| ** Production  $\\,$ ($x$)** | **$24$** | **$27$** | **$30$** | **$31$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ** Price per $kg$  ($y$) ** | $2$ | $1.6$ | $1.4$ | $1.4$ |\n\nThe table above shows wheat production, in tons, and the average price of a kilogram of flour, in dollars, for a given country over the last four years. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.007,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.   \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-4-0.1x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(24-28)^{2}+(27-28)^{2}+(30-28)^{2}+(31-28)^{2} \\\\  & =16+1+4+9 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.007}{30}}≈0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.1\\pm 0.04\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169095 :answer-field/correct "answer-8ac845ba-d2b