-x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-4.5)^{2}+(4-4.5)^{2}+(5-4.5)^{2}+(7-4.5)^{2} \\\\  & =6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.456}{13}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-1\\pm 0.5\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" "answer-337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" "answer-812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" "answer-c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" "answer-a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" :answer/id #uuid "95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.8\\pm 0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" :answer/id #uuid "337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" :answer/id #uuid "c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad" :answer/id #uuid "a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" :question/id #uuid "c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" :question/math-academy-id "q-159518" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.338}{45}}≈0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.5\\pm 0.3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09" :answer-field/id #uuid "086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" "answer-d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" "answer-82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" "answer-9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" "answer-8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" :answer/id #uuid "4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" :answer/id #uuid "d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" :answer/id #uuid "82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198" :answer/id #uuid "8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" :question/id #uuid "939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159541" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{7.7}{40}}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.7\\pm 1.9\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" "answer-0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" "answer-4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" "answer-a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" "answer-d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" :answer/id #uuid "770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" :answer/id #uuid "0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" :answer/id #uuid "4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931" :answer/id #uuid "d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 2.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173218 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population. \n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x.\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$  are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} -  \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i,$ and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following: \n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as \n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize, \n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x,$\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1,$ and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the slope parameter $\\beta_1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173229 :tutorial/content "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed by fitting the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the so-called **mean squared error** of the model, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i).\n$$\n\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2.$\n\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173228 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our slope parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_1}.$\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i).$\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nFor each $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ let\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i,\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and \n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2.$ The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left( \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i , \\sigma^2\\right).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_1 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\: \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}} \\right).\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2,$ the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2,\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2.$\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_1$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_1 -\\beta_1}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] }\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom. \n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173249 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy},$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}\\cdot 0+{\\beta }_{1}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n\n- For the variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172162 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186165033 :answer-field/choices 17592186165034] [:db/retract 17592186165033 :answer-field/choices 17592186165035] [:db/retract 17592186165033 :answer-field/choices 17592186165036] [:db/retract 17592186165033 :answer-field/choices 17592186165037] [:db/retract 17592186165033 :answer-field/choices 17592186165038] {:db/id 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" "question-7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" :question/id #uuid "e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346662" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{C}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{C}(x)$ of a set $C$ equals $1$ if $x\\in C$ and $0$ if $x\\notin C.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(x)=1[x\\in C].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(-1)=1[-1\\in C]=1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c55f3e7-608f-546e-9238-bec098222d4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c55f3e7-608f-546e-9238-bec098222d4f" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c55f3e7-608f-546e-9238-bec098222d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a42e783-06ac-540f-a993-5fee32a5d1a7" "answer-c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" "answer-905baf65-bc02-52ab-a96c-06c275629272" "answer-c64a4829-6ad8-5110-9db6-5fd9a585d15a" "answer-d3d06cfb-9a89-5639-8808-7411f0dd598c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a42e783-06ac-540f-a993-5fee32a5d1a7" :answer/id #uuid "7a42e783-06ac-540f-a993-5fee32a5d1a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[-1=C]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" :answer/id #uuid "c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-905baf65-bc02-52ab-a96c-06c275629272" :answer/id #uuid "905baf65-bc02-52ab-a96c-06c275629272" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[-1\\notin \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c64a4829-6ad8-5110-9db6-5fd9a585d15a" :answer/id #uuid "c64a4829-6ad8-5110-9db6-5fd9a585d15a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[C\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3d06cfb-9a89-5639-8808-7411f0dd598c" :answer/id #uuid "d3d06cfb-9a89-5639-8808-7411f0dd598c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}\\in -1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" :question/id #uuid "7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346663" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{0,5,10\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{D}(7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{D}(x)$ of a set $D$ equals $1$ if $x\\in D$ and $0$ if $x\\notin D.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(x)=1[x\\in D].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(7)=1[7\\in D]=1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd8b1eb9-905f-5eaa-b94d-45d4a5b08f18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd8b1eb9-905f-5eaa-b94d-45d4a5b08f18" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8b1eb9-905f-5eaa-b94d-45d4a5b08f18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e5a4ce9-38a3-50c2-ab37-2b31fa255565" "answer-929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" "answer-caeec9e3-866d-5050-ab7c-4b6736d7497b" "answer-3f98e784-15fe-5da6-bf43-5d7bde990e34" "answer-34d52dde-78fc-56d2-a22f-16743942f394"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e5a4ce9-38a3-50c2-ab37-2b31fa255565" :answer/id #uuid "3e5a4ce9-38a3-50c2-ab37-2b31fa255565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[7=D]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" :answer/id #uuid "929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caeec9e3-866d-5050-ab7c-4b6736d7497b" :answer/id #uuid "caeec9e3-866d-5050-ab7c-4b6736d7497b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[7\\notin \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f98e784-15fe-5da6-bf43-5d7bde990e34" :answer/id #uuid "3f98e784-15fe-5da6-bf43-5d7bde990e34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[D\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34d52dde-78fc-56d2-a22f-16743942f394" :answer/id #uuid "34d52dde-78fc-56d2-a22f-16743942f394" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[\\{0,5,10\\}\\in 7]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" "question-e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" :question/id #uuid "f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346677" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-8>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[4>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9+0+0+4+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-034b7137-7dce-507f-82b0-3f725c6f8c4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-034b7137-7dce-507f-82b0-3f725c6f8c4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "034b7137-7dce-507f-82b0-3f725c6f8c4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59" :answer-field/choices ["answer-156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59" :answer/id #uuid "156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" :question/id #uuid "e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-347438" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1$ equals $2{x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $1$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nadds $2{x}_{i}$ for nonnegative values and $1$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-6\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[-1\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[4\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[7\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1 & =1+1+2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =1+1+0+8+14 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-edb32b44-fa3a-53b5-9c67-b4cbe35e057e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-edb32b44-fa3a-53b5-9c67-b4cbe35e057e" :answer-field/id #uuid "edb32b44-fa3a-53b5-9c67-b4cbe35e057e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba" :answer/id #uuid "dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167304 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5\\in B]?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186165033 :answer-field/correct "answer-fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" "answer-23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" "answer-fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" "answer-6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" "answer-0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" :answer/id #uuid "6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{5}(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" :answer/id #uuid "23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{5}(B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer/id #uuid "fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{B}(5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" :answer/id #uuid "6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{B}(6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71" :answer/id #uuid "0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{\\{2,4,6,8\\}}(B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-465539fa-aa06-5c88-a039-a2b61aa93569" :activity/id #uuid "465539fa-aa06-5c88-a039-a2b61aa93569" :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050260 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" "step-bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" "step-2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" "step-0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" "step-3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39"] :activity/first-step "step-3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" :step/id #uuid "3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" :step/content "question-e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" :step/next "step-bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" :step/id #uuid "bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" :step/content "question-7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" :step/next "step-2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" :step/id #uuid "2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" :step/content "question-f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" :step/next "step-0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" :step/id #uuid "0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" :step/content "question-e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" :step/next "step-3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39" :step/id #uuid "3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39" :step/content 17592186167304 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186154010 :answer-field/choices 17592186154011] [:db/retract 17592186154010 :answer-field/choices 17592186154012] [:db/retract 17592186154010 :answer-field/choices 17592186154013] [:db/retract 17592186154010 :answer-field/choices 17592186154014] [:db/retract 17592186154010 :answer-field/choices 17592186154015] {:db/id 17592186157378 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a0d0470d-b24d-5636-b2cb-b9c005b7e95d" "question-1f3c3035-69e9-5d03-a0fc-4433cacd4260" "question-2b4b8cea-e134-5e94-b33a-7dc117bff583"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157198 :question/problem "The graph of $y=-f(x-2)+3,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}1)$ - Translation by $3$ units up\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}3)$ - Translation by $2$ units to the right\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Translation by $2$ units to the left\n\nWhat could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-f(x-2)+3,$ we could apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(x-2).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)\\mathbin{:}$ Reflect $y=f(x-2)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(x-2).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=-f(x-2)$ by $3$ units up to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(x-2)+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\qquad$ $(\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2) \\to (\\textrm{T}1).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157319 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1714eddf-429e-5900-b54a-c409f35eac75" "question-044825ff-2a34-548f-8e31-b6e40bc76ccd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157123 :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=-f(x+3).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c57e0ebec3c6dcc4b6d1b72a8dde395fbe4a90801c18718fc36edf84469af4f.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $3$ units $y=f(x+3).$\n\n![](images/07/0770e092f1d63c0a9c4416f92fa6e543ffcc47bcd78375f5b835b64df3ef691a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the  $x$- axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x+3).$\n\n![](images/5f/5fde1d3543593322ad5caebbf28d4623571032e35c516dd051583294f452e679.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157379 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-689e7bfc-91b5-5578-9523-c9029e296a11" "question-f73ff2c0-b98a-5833-8e43-d855656798f9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157199 :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=f\\left(-3x\\right).$\n\n![](images/7a/7a0aac61b989b6aac26352c2f3faa05fc87ef37ec8ec9e120c83eabd213ed245.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the minus sign next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(3x\\right).$\n\n![](images/c8/c858bf111c2dbc55aa3308276d5ee93aaa0f7a0b4e5b4b69e749f552bd548d57.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right).$\n\n![](images/25/25bcd904d279c4e054e5d66b731ef294a3c1441d28e916c2824f99b9f16a3b7e.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157380 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b3b851a-20c6-5158-b86d-a090b5acc8fc" "question-269fb630-a40e-5592-b4e3-251a076c8d27" "question-d8c07848-a1dd-5eb7-8320-0b4777fcd645" "question-95bf6a06-ae4e-5d38-8c20-f91522b5fa5e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157200 :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=3f\\left(-x+1\\right)?$\n\n![](images/ca/ca4445101ef048b82262dcca5a91d249bcf1c3172417def297ddd45cb59b9053.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the negative sign next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-x\\right)$ (remember that multiplication comes *before* subtraction in the order of operations).\n\n![](images/6c/6cde0cf55bd7a80cfb717eac89288dc99cede1b8456a0140d3e30c741958793d.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-(x-1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/80/803eb49b759eb31716470db93878fd2f3b73aaa0d8085bacd4be5a5f6706254d.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/37/3781afe1b28d36c73f2831461fee136d93a6bfe4439f47bb96dfb462a62677e2.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0d0470d-b24d-5636-b2cb-b9c005b7e95d" :question/id #uuid "a0d0470d-b24d-5636-b2cb-b9c005b7e95d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97783" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(-(x+5))$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $5$ units to the left\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-(x+5)),$ we can apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Reflect $y=f(x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-x)$ to the left by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-(x+5)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e9f64e3-d6b1-578a-a830-b151ec93c86a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e9f64e3-d6b1-578a-a830-b151ec93c86a" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e9f64e3-d6b1-578a-a830-b151ec93c86a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f97c969-cb30-5fff-a90b-aa56b316a079" :answer-field/choices ["answer-551368a1-5a2b-53df-a888-581d3b746c74" "answer-83f8bd03-8875-57a2-9f70-253fd82cf18c" "answer-5573eca1-d3ad-5818-9703-94fc1488f674" "answer-8f97c969-cb30-5fff-a90b-aa56b316a079" "answer-37bcc534-503f-59d4-9a82-79df30b5d695"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-551368a1-5a2b-53df-a888-581d3b746c74" :answer/id #uuid "551368a1-5a2b-53df-a888-581d3b746c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83f8bd03-8875-57a2-9f70-253fd82cf18c" :answer/id #uuid "83f8bd03-8875-57a2-9f70-253fd82cf18c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5573eca1-d3ad-5818-9703-94fc1488f674" :answer/id #uuid "5573eca1-d3ad-5818-9703-94fc1488f674" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f97c969-cb30-5fff-a90b-aa56b316a079" :answer/id #uuid "8f97c969-cb30-5fff-a90b-aa56b316a079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37bcc534-503f-59d4-9a82-79df30b5d695" :answer/id #uuid "37bcc534-503f-59d4-9a82-79df30b5d695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157629 :question/problem "The graph of $y=-f(2x)+4,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translation by $4$ units up\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translation by $4$ units down\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-f(2x)+4,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1):$ Translate $y=-f(2x)$ up by $4$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x)+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186154010 :answer-field/correct "answer-afd3f0e2-369a-516c-b862-b1b6ce58050e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cef0025-ab5d-58b2-abc8-d28529eb5dd1" "answer-290e7843-220a-5a89-8fdb-575b887de178" "answer-ec477bfb-f29c-5738-93e7-ba81e2c54bcc" "answer-afd3f0e2-369a-516c-b862-b1b6ce58050e" "answer-58b4d56e-4ca9-5cda-b97d-7a3cf9baf086"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cef0025-ab5d-58b2-abc8-d28529eb5dd1" :answer/id #uuid "5cef0025-ab5d-58b2-abc8-d28529eb5dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-290e7843-220a-5a89-8fdb-575b887de178" :answer/id #uuid "290e7843-220a-5a89-8fdb-575b887de178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec477bfb-f29c-5738-93e7-ba81e2c54bcc" :answer/id #uuid "ec477bfb-f29c-5738-93e7-ba81e2c54bcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afd3f0e2-369a-516c-b862-b1b6ce58050e" :answer/id #uuid "afd3f0e2-369a-516c-b862-b1b6ce58050e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58b4d56e-4ca9-5cda-b97d-7a3cf9baf086" :answer/id #uuid "58b4d56e-4ca9-5cda-b97d-7a3cf9baf086" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f3c3035-69e9-5d03-a0fc-4433cacd4260" :question/id #uuid "1f3c3035-69e9-5d03-a0fc-4433cacd4260" :question/math-academy-id "q-97793" :question/problem "The graph of $y=-3f(4x),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-3f(4x),$ we could apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(4x)$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=3f(4x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-3f(4x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50c831bd-2f30-5cb8-8cf7-31fdc3d03f6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50c831bd-2f30-5cb8-8cf7-31fdc3d03f6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "50c831bd-2f30-5cb8-8cf7-31fdc3d03f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09c17a4a-4f53-57c9-8f3c-4badcbcbacd6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8edc4dd2-11b2-5482-9115-874fe418a3a0" "answer-a7e37100-4c73-555e-917a-6323823839b4" "answer-3a37e768-dde7-5db7-8cba-08c9fe455725" "answer-09c17a4a-4f53-57c9-8f3c-4badcbcbacd6" "answer-fdd525da-9b20-59fa-85be-c6ccda816d58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8edc4dd2-11b2-5482-9115-874fe418a3a0" :answer/id #uuid "8edc4dd2-11b2-5482-9115-874fe418a3a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7e37100-4c73-555e-917a-6323823839b4" :answer/id #uuid "a7e37100-4c73-555e-917a-6323823839b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a37e768-dde7-5db7-8cba-08c9fe455725" :answer/id #uuid "3a37e768-dde7-5db7-8cba-08c9fe455725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09c17a4a-4f53-57c9-8f3c-4badcbcbacd6" :answer/id #uuid "09c17a4a-4f53-57c9-8f3c-4badcbcbacd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdd525da-9b20-59fa-85be-c6ccda816d58" :answer/id #uuid "fdd525da-9b20-59fa-85be-c6ccda816d58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b4b8cea-e134-5e94-b33a-7dc117bff583" :question/id #uuid "2b4b8cea-e134-5e94-b33a-7dc117bff583" :question/math-academy-id "q-97785" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(-2x)-1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $1$ unit down\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-2x)-1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2(-x))=f(-2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-2x)$ down by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-2x)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0990bc9f-b980-589b-b932-ab04f3aca435"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0990bc9f-b980-589b-b932-ab04f3ac