:answer/value "\\{119,123\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23a4ac02-1db6-583f-82e6-b5db013364aa" :answer/id #uuid "23a4ac02-1db6-583f-82e6-b5db013364aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∅" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3ebe755-cc28-56a9-a6f9-b3ea716cdfc2" :question/id #uuid "e3ebe755-cc28-56a9-a6f9-b3ea716cdfc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36187" :question/problem "If $A=\\{3,5,7,19,21,115\\}$ and $B=\\{2,3,4,7,16,19,21,22\\},$ then $A\\cup B=$" :question/worked-solution "The set $A\\cup B$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $A$ or $B,$ or both. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & =\\{3,5,7,19,21,115\\}\\cup \\{2,3,4,7,16,19,21,22\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,3,4,5,7,16,19,21,22,115\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac450825-0a43-5655-9b1b-af418583e6b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac450825-0a43-5655-9b1b-af418583e6b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac450825-0a43-5655-9b1b-af418583e6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a7306b0-7e39-58dc-8d6a-5c02f3a160f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a7306b0-7e39-58dc-8d6a-5c02f3a160f8" "answer-d9399757-788a-5b06-a74e-af23406c7392" "answer-eae0bc31-6206-5abc-b4da-1a42e5fed347" "answer-bf0053d6-a254-5c1e-99ea-5ee6dcb07e88" "answer-10b45b2c-b1e5-51df-9233-681766e27744"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a7306b0-7e39-58dc-8d6a-5c02f3a160f8" :answer/id #uuid "4a7306b0-7e39-58dc-8d6a-5c02f3a160f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,3,4,5,7,16,19,21,22,115\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9399757-788a-5b06-a74e-af23406c7392" :answer/id #uuid "d9399757-788a-5b06-a74e-af23406c7392" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,3,4,…,114,115\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae0bc31-6206-5abc-b4da-1a42e5fed347" :answer/id #uuid "eae0bc31-6206-5abc-b4da-1a42e5fed347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,4,16,22,115\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf0053d6-a254-5c1e-99ea-5ee6dcb07e88" :answer/id #uuid "bf0053d6-a254-5c1e-99ea-5ee6dcb07e88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,4,5,16,19,22,115\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10b45b2c-b1e5-51df-9233-681766e27744" :answer/id #uuid "10b45b2c-b1e5-51df-9233-681766e27744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{3,7,19,21\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7be71bb-2a94-5959-b6e1-bd279930afab" :question/id #uuid "b7be71bb-2a94-5959-b6e1-bd279930afab" :question/math-academy-id "q-102147" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be the set of all non-negative integer powers of $3$ and the set of all multiples of $4$ greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $X\\cup Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The non-negative integer powers of $3$ are\n\n$$\nX=\\{1,3,9,27,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nY=\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $X\\cup Y$ is the *union* of $X$ and $Y,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in either $X$ or $Y,$ or both. In other words, $X\\cup Y$ denotes the set of natural numbers that are either powers of $3$ or multiples of  $4.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cup Y & =\\{1,3,9,27,…\\}\\cup \\{4,8,12,16,20,24,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,4,8,9,12,16,20,24,27,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a097d8b4-61d7-5c24-b4fc-50630068161e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a097d8b4-61d7-5c24-b4fc-50630068161e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a097d8b4-61d7-5c24-b4fc-50630068161e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f3ce74f-3cd9-5ddc-870f-ba877c7553c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdbc25d6-14b6-50fd-a0ba-7e894e123ebf" "answer-2f3ce74f-3cd9-5ddc-870f-ba877c7553c2" "answer-d79beda6-776f-5231-b900-195a05c1e7fa" "answer-6d532b24-3d74-5a1e-9f87-6c1093e9a0ab" "answer-69419d5e-cec8-59b3-9be5-476325804ea8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdbc25d6-14b6-50fd-a0ba-7e894e123ebf" :answer/id #uuid "fdbc25d6-14b6-50fd-a0ba-7e894e123ebf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3,4,6,8,9,12,15,18,20,21,25,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f3ce74f-3cd9-5ddc-870f-ba877c7553c2" :answer/id #uuid "2f3ce74f-3cd9-5ddc-870f-ba877c7553c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3,4,8,9,12,16,20,24,27,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d79beda6-776f-5231-b900-195a05c1e7fa" :answer/id #uuid "d79beda6-776f-5231-b900-195a05c1e7fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d532b24-3d74-5a1e-9f87-6c1093e9a0ab" :answer/id #uuid "6d532b24-3d74-5a1e-9f87-6c1093e9a0ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,4,8,12,16,20,24,28,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69419d5e-cec8-59b3-9be5-476325804ea8" :answer/id #uuid "69419d5e-cec8-59b3-9be5-476325804ea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{3,4,6,8,9,12,15,16,18,20,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234433 :question/worked-solution "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA=\\{4,8,12,16,20,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB=\\{5,10,15,20,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A\\cup B$ is the *union* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both. In other words, $A\\cup B$ denotes the set of natural numbers that are either multiple of $4$ or multiple of $5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & =\\{4,8,12,16,20,…\\}\\cup \\{5,10,15,20,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{4,5,8,10,12,15,16,20,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-016e34fa-e716-5145-99b7-e642198e7552"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-016e34fa-e716-5145-99b7-e642198e7552" :answer-field/id #uuid "016e34fa-e716-5145-99b7-e642198e7552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-510fcf9e-dd61-537d-a97f-bef963a126ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fc3a318-f1ec-557e-93d1-000d38d24f1f" "answer-510fcf9e-dd61-537d-a97f-bef963a126ad" "answer-5b98ebe7-4274-5a37-944e-0946b674c52b" "answer-42e85ab6-b6b8-5b56-a9f6-9a4f3ba83c4c" "answer-da3fae6a-8970-5e1e-b559-e96788554277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fc3a318-f1ec-557e-93d1-000d38d24f1f" :answer/id #uuid "8fc3a318-f1ec-557e-93d1-000d38d24f1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{20,40,60,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-510fcf9e-dd61-537d-a97f-bef963a126ad" :answer/id #uuid "510fcf9e-dd61-537d-a97f-bef963a126ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{4,5,8,10,12,15,16,20,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b98ebe7-4274-5a37-944e-0946b674c52b" :answer/id #uuid "5b98ebe7-4274-5a37-944e-0946b674c52b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{4,8,12,16,20,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42e85ab6-b6b8-5b56-a9f6-9a4f3ba83c4c" :answer/id #uuid "42e85ab6-b6b8-5b56-a9f6-9a4f3ba83c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{5,10,15,20,…\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da3fae6a-8970-5e1e-b559-e96788554277" :answer/id #uuid "da3fae6a-8970-5e1e-b559-e96788554277" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∅" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158472 :tutorial/content "The **union** of two sets ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) denoted ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) is the set of all elements belonging to either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\nFor example, consider the sets ![](images/32/3228232fb4c16493d5268b308ede8727a84c20297d9b216a74db7b537011c704.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d576c7a3682975f73724c0fbddaf962449049968b852a28434102128ffa8a96.svg) We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/8351db4817ac8b0d2991fe8ef09ee1f2583c84cb772114ef0614a0ef96c96389.png)\n\nThe union ![](images/58/58a13b2e88b95997772105b179139f2bb67105050519a451c17b838337cb4632.svg) consists of all the elements that are in ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. To represent ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) we will shade in all the area that is contained within either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\n![](images/e9/e912fe53d530ac47245c916bd16f1758df487ded32da88abfab253ed1ee0cc63.png)\n\nThe elements ![](images/0e/0e9479f799610ac47cadcc6890bb35f5336c08c9e2827384fd299138b7e4c558.svg) are in either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. Therefore, the union of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) is\n\n![](images/dc/dc7e001d55359f56770a3cdd382a7318d77cbb12b260141399192d14953b4146.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg) belongs to both ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) but we list it only once in ![](images/bb/bbb285be6a3e138649aa4a514c34823a6b39d094dca3427eb34eb4a326854fcf.svg) In a set, each distinct element is written only once." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158478 :tutorial/content "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize unions, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a union.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the union $A \\cup B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both.\n\nSo, we will start with the elements of $A,$ and then go through the elements of $B$ to look for any elements that we do not already have. Remember that we are looking for elements that we do *not* already have in $A.$\n\n- For the element $b \\in B,$ we have $b \\in A. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $d \\in B,$ we have $d \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $e \\in B,$ we have $e \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nSo, to get the union, we need to take the elements of $A$ and also include $d$ and $e.$\n\nTherefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA \\cup B = \\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157483 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/id #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" "answer-00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" "answer-8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" "answer-e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/id #uuid "9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/id #uuid "00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/id #uuid "8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/id #uuid "e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/id #uuid "b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049265 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" "step-86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" "step-9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2"] :activity/first-step "step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/id #uuid "67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/content 17592186367463 :step/next "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/id #uuid "1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/content "question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :step/next "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/id #uuid "83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/content 17592186269200 :step/next "step-86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :step/id #uuid "86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :step/content 17592186530454 :step/next "step-9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2" :step/id #uuid "9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2" :step/content 17592186530493 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d4eb2e58-0270-5bef-ba01-92f957f243d8" :activity/id #uuid "d4eb2e58-0270-5bef-ba01-92f957f243d8" :activity/title "The Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049530 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-1cc91ec0-2b1b-58e6-ae97-b3acdb0e54b9" "step-7cdecdff-864c-55db-a059-af7ac85b33ec" "step-883124dd-93a1-51f7-80e0-e24a71d24b25" "step-7225349a-cd1a-56df-a96a-edd346e21d36" "step-806fac15-2f54-54de-a049-aba5331700f0"] :activity/first-step "step-1cc91ec0-2b1b-58e6-ae97-b3acdb0e54b9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1cc91ec0-2b1b-58e6-ae97-b3acdb0e54b9" :step/id #uuid "1cc91ec0-2b1b-58e6-ae97-b3acdb0e54b9" :step/content 17592186129751 :step/next "step-7cdecdff-864c-55db-a059-af7ac85b33ec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7cdecdff-864c-55db-a059-af7ac85b33ec" :step/id #uuid "7cdecdff-864c-55db-a059-af7ac85b33ec" :step/content 17592186281332 :step/next "step-883124dd-93a1-51f7-80e0-e24a71d24b25" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-883124dd-93a1-51f7-80e0-e24a71d24b25" :step/id #uuid "883124dd-93a1-51f7-80e0-e24a71d24b25" :step/content 17592186424180 :step/next "step-7225349a-cd1a-56df-a96a-edd346e21d36" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7225349a-cd1a-56df-a96a-edd346e21d36" :step/id #uuid "7225349a-cd1a-56df-a96a-edd346e21d36" :step/content 17592186401275 :step/next "step-806fac15-2f54-54de-a049-aba5331700f0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-806fac15-2f54-54de-a049-aba5331700f0" :step/id #uuid "806fac15-2f54-54de-a049-aba5331700f0" :step/content 17592186281342 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-09d71431-cc0e-5153-8350-c0a86eafb77b" "question-758c8bb2-c32e-5b6a-b65e-f02ce4ad0d8b" "question-f380d0a4-2ab4-5ffd-9f65-88568a9b06d4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-09d71431-cc0e-5153-8350-c0a86eafb77b" :question/id #uuid "09d71431-cc0e-5153-8350-c0a86eafb77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3047" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{45{y}^{2}}$ given that $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $45{y}^{2}$ as the product $9{y}^{2}\\cdot 5$ which contains a perfect square $(9{y}^{2}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{45{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{y}^{2}\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ |3y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot y\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-391f389b-05b6-594c-95d0-cca64d0e6b52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-391f389b-05b6-594c-95d0-cca64d0e6b52" :answer-field/id #uuid "391f389b-05b6-594c-95d0-cca64d0e6b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-369e7a6b-727f-5c71-8be5-8892046afcfb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78a5d1c0-2297-5d46-9446-2060429a75d2" "answer-d1a5b4b0-a22e-503c-b26d-aee6391ea028" "answer-369e7a6b-727f-5c71-8be5-8892046afcfb" "answer-0431e39d-71d1-5768-88be-409ca33f6c7b" "answer-889645be-9d28-546a-9122-d1ad0f6e84d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78a5d1c0-2297-5d46-9446-2060429a75d2" :answer/id #uuid "78a5d1c0-2297-5d46-9446-2060429a75d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1a5b4b0-a22e-503c-b26d-aee6391ea028" :answer/id #uuid "d1a5b4b0-a22e-503c-b26d-aee6391ea028" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-369e7a6b-727f-5c71-8be5-8892046afcfb" :answer/id #uuid "369e7a6b-727f-5c71-8be5-8892046afcfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3y\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0431e39d-71d1-5768-88be-409ca33f6c7b" :answer/id #uuid "0431e39d-71d1-5768-88be-409ca33f6c7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}^{2}\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-889645be-9d28-546a-9122-d1ad0f6e84d6" :answer/id #uuid "889645be-9d28-546a-9122-d1ad0f6e84d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5y\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-758c8bb2-c32e-5b6a-b65e-f02ce4ad0d8b" :question/id #uuid "758c8bb2-c32e-5b6a-b65e-f02ce4ad0d8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-29342" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}}$ given that $a\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}$ as the product $16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}\\cdot 2a$ which contains a perfect square $(16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4{a}^{3}{b}^{4})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4{a}^{3}{b}^{4})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ |4{a}^{3}{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ |4|\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA number raised to the power of four is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{b}^{4}|={b}^{4}$ and $|{a}^{3}|={a}^{3}.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot {a}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4{a}^{3}{b}^{4}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aef36574-d26a-51ae-8adf-50d31d43f76c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aef36574-d26a-51ae-8adf-50d31d43f76c" :answer-field/id #uuid "aef36574-d26a-51ae-8adf-50d31d43f76c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ac23f07-19eb-5a0a-91fe-2143e303a9c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3fbfc23-489f-58fe-b8ff-e2ebc4569716" "answer-e7e84788-8981-5728-a6a0-aba9de9a572d" "answer-0ac23f07-19eb-5a0a-91fe-2143e303a9c2" "answer-8a26477d-d557-5b83-8ec1-5e390a04ff66" "answer-cec1e7d9-d8c0-5441-9601-896e2db22b3d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3fbfc23-489f-58fe-b8ff-e2ebc4569716" :answer/id #uuid "c3fbfc23-489f-58fe-b8ff-e2ebc4569716" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{b}^{3}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7e84788-8981-5728-a6a0-aba9de9a572d" :answer/id #uuid "e7e84788-8981-5728-a6a0-aba9de9a572d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{a}^{2}{b}^{4}\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ac23f07-19eb-5a0a-91fe-2143e303a9c2" :answer/id #uuid "0ac23f07-19eb-5a0a-91fe-2143e303a9c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{a}^{3}{b}^{4}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a26477d-d557-5b83-8ec1-5e390a04ff66" :answer/id #uuid "8a26477d-d557-5b83-8ec1-5e390a04ff66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{b}^{4}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cec1e7d9-d8c0-5441-9601-896e2db22b3d" :answer/id #uuid "cec1e7d9-d8c0-5441-9601-896e2db22b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8a{b}^{4}\\sqrt{a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f380d0a4-2ab4-5ffd-9f65-88568a9b06d4" :question/id #uuid "f380d0a4-2ab4-5ffd-9f65-88568a9b06d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33483" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first write $20{n}^{5}$ as the product $4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n$ which contains a perfect square $(4{n}^{4}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2{n}^{2}\\sqrt{5n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5f773159-8463-5e37-81f8-8746b86bfcf3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f773159-8463-5e37-81f8-8746b86bfcf3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f773159-8463-5e37-81f8-8746b86bfcf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c8d00de-5cfb-5d7d-b6ce-1a3fcf580c19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-493f354b-b71f-58ef-b5af-c43b305893c7" "answer-2c8d00de-5cfb-5d7d-b6ce-1a3fcf580c19" "answer-8a439d62-6628-5381-a609-99d4cc463d9e" "answer-c8dc9bd6-1c8a-5517-9d4e-a5d506cda766" "answer-ddeb1d91-9f54-55f6-b72d-fd60d991fa39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493f354b-b71f-58ef-b5af-c43b305893c7" :answer/id #uuid "493f354b-b71f-58ef-b5af-c43b305893c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2n\\sqrt{5n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c8d00de-5cfb-5d7d-b6ce-1a3fcf580c19" :answer/id #uuid "2c8d00de-5cfb-5d7d-b6ce-1a3fcf580c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{n}^{2}\\sqrt{5n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a439d62-6628-5381-a609-99d4cc463d9e" :answer/id #uuid "8a439d62-6628-5381-a609-99d4cc463d9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5n\\sqrt{2n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8dc9bd6-1c8a-5517-9d4e-a5d506cda766" :answer/id #uuid "c8dc9bd6-1c8a-5517-9d4e-a5d506cda766" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{n}^{2}\\sqrt{2n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddeb1d91-9f54-55f6-b72d-fd60d991fa39" :answer/id #uuid "ddeb1d91-9f54-55f6-b72d-fd60d991fa39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{n}^{2}\\sqrt{5n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-18d4c92e-e0a8-5b92-a68e-b9cd79663667" :activity/id #uuid "18d4c92e-e0a8-5b92-a68e-b9cd79663667" :activity/title "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Product Rule" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050496 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-4793c529-2358-59fb-98fc-407d9b60139f" "step-b80055f6-9aac-5f82-8760-3f10697f55c5" "step-9070e2ab-f493-5ae1-ae96-aa294b03d716"] :activity/first-step "step-4793c529-2358-59fb-98fc-407d9b60139f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4793c529-2358-59fb-98fc-407d9b60139f" :step/id #uuid "4793c529-2358-59fb-98fc-407d9b60139f" :step/content "question-09d71431-cc0e-5153-8350-c0a86eafb77b" :step/next "step-b80055f6-9aac-5f82-8760-3f10697f55c5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b80055f6-9aac-5f82-8760-3f10697f55c5" :step/id #uuid "b80055f6-9aac-5f82-8760-3f10697f55c5" :step/content "question-758c8bb2-c32e-5b6a-b65e-f02ce4ad0d8b" :step/next "step-9070e2ab-f493-5ae1-ae96-aa294b03d716" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9070e2ab-f493-5ae1-ae96-aa294b03d716" :step/id #uuid "9070e2ab-f493-5ae1-ae96-aa294b03d716" :step/content "question-f380d0a4-2ab4-5ffd-9f65-88568a9b06d4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-df749831-59f4-54ca-a246-70aff57225d8" :activity/id #uuid "df749831-59f4-54ca-a246-70aff57225d8" :activity/title "Quiz 1" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-9b008ed9-f8e9-5df1-88f2-340b688a70cd" "step-0eab5434-b5a9-5a0f-ba85-3d586bfabf06" "step-f7fe91bf-0a46-51a0-9a78-e31622e1048a" "step-9c67f234-edd9-5f68-9e18-65bf94579004"] :activity/first-step "step-9b008ed9-f8e9-5df1-88f2-340b688a70cd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9b008ed9-f8e9-5df1-88f2-340b688a70cd" :step/id #uuid "9b008ed9-f8e9-5df1-88f2-340b688a70cd" :step/content 17592186421456 :step/next "step-0eab5434-b5a9-5a0f-ba85-3d586bfabf06" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0eab5434-b5a9-5a0f-ba85-3d586bfabf06" :step/id #uuid "0eab5434-b5a9-5a0f-ba85-3d586bfabf06" :step/content 17592186326896 :step/next "step-f7fe91bf-0a46-51a0-9a78-e31622e1048a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7fe91bf-0a46-51a0-9a78-e31622e1048a" :step/id #uuid "f7fe91bf-0a46-51a0-9a78-e31622e1048a" :step/content 17592186162663 :step/next "step-9c67f234-edd9-5f68-9e18-65bf94579004" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9c67f234-edd9-5f68-9e18-65bf94579004" :step/id #uuid "9c67f234-edd9-5f68-9e18-65bf94579004" :step/content 17592186369720 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78" :question/id #uuid "e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78" :question/math-academy-id "q-127310" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\,{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64fabadb-f80e-5526-b940-02e3d7342ff0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64fabadb-f80e-5526-b940-02e3d7342ff0" :answer-field/id #uuid "64fabadb-f80e-5526-b940-02e3d7342ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e59702b-e79a-5b04-9309-46a6ac9eac76" "answer-f58d9465-726d-5a95-9c7d-efc84289b65d" "answer-1fa26e4c-314f-5b70-a15a-1830ec32c897" "answer-61157c7e-b646-58f6-a043-51dc356ac96d" "answer-bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e59702b-e79a-5b04-9309-46a6ac9eac76" :answer/id #uuid "8e59702b-e79a-5b04-9309-46a6ac9eac76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f58d9465-726d-5a95-9c7d-efc84289b65d" :answer/id #uuid "f58d9465-726d-5a95-9c7d-efc84289b65d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa26e4c-314f-5b70-a15a-1830ec32c897" :answer/id #uuid "1fa26e4c-314f-5b70-a15a-1830ec32c897" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61157c7e-b646-58f6-a043-51dc356ac96d" :answer/id #uuid "61157c7e-b646-58f6-a043-51dc356ac96d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f" :answer/id #uuid "bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6ad25cf5-915c-59ed-8b39-5e9a6998c5a8" :activity/id #uuid "6ad25cf5-915c-59ed-8b39-5e9a6998c5a8" :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050694 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-b61a74fa-7b3b-5a75-9b36-4b9533c9a9cd" "step-4ff89872-91e6-53c4-9d19-3490a585a972" "step-294740c4-a895-510b-b2f2-8fc11a1beb8f" "step-989b214e-cc0a-554a-a40f-d6e57d83e034" "step-3daca5bb-72c1-504c-aefd-215eeb4c35f9"] :activity/first-step "step-b61a74fa-7b3b-5a75-9b36-4b9533c9a9cd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b61a74fa-7b3b-5a75-9b36-4b9533c9a9cd" :step/id #uuid "b61a74fa-7b3b-5a75-9b36-4b9533c9a9cd" :step/content 17592186424519 :step/next "step-4ff89872-91e6-53c4-9d19-3490a585a972" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4ff89872-91e6-53c4-9d19-3490a585a972" :step/id #uuid "4ff89872-91e6-53c4-9d19-3490a585a972" :step/content "question-e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78" :step/next "step-294740c4-a895-510b-b2f2-8fc11a1beb8f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-294740c4-a895-510b-b2f2-8fc11a1beb8f" :step/id #uuid "294740c4-a895-510b-b2f2-8fc11a1beb8f" :step/content 17592186481405 :step/next "step-989b214e-cc0a-554a-a40f-d6e57d83e034" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-989b214e-cc0a-554a-a40f-d6e57d83e034" :step/id #uuid "989b214e-cc0a-554a-a40f-d6e57d83e034" :step/content 17592186424518 :step/next "step-3daca5bb-72c1-504c-aefd-215eeb4c35f9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3daca5bb-72c1-504c-aefd-215eeb4c35f9" :step/id #uuid "3daca5bb-72c1-504c-aefd-215eeb4c35f9" :step/content 17592186424520 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189761] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189762] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189763] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189764] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189765] {:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" "question-851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" "question-24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" "question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192387 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" "question-14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" "question-a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" "question-4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" "question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192388 :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/id #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159563" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/id #uuid "08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" "answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" "answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" "answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/id #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/id #uuid "868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/id #uuid "460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/id #uuid "b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}