iven by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=5\\pm 2.5\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" "answer-19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" "answer-3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" "answer-ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" "answer-60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/id #uuid "dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/id #uuid "19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/id #uuid "3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" :answer/id #uuid "ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c" :answer/id #uuid "60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 3.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" :question/id #uuid "24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-159566" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.6-3.9x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=12.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.921)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.005}=2.921.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.921\\cdot \\sqrt{2.1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{85})}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=12.6\\pm 1.9\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/id #uuid "c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer-field/choices ["answer-402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" "answer-3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" "answer-83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" "answer-9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/id #uuid "402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/id #uuid "3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/id #uuid "83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/id #uuid "9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f